  "summary": "Ten preprints and one paper scrutinize benchmark design, baselines, and reliability of perturbation, deconvolution, and histology-based prediction models.",
  "synthesis": "Recent items center on evaluation methodology for predictive models in single-cell and spatial biology rather than new model architectures. Several benchmarks make evaluation assumptions explicit: InverseBench reports ridge accuracy falling from 0.959 on a proxy to 0.445 on K562 growth data, and a CRISPRi study shows that averaging cells per perturbation label discards perturbation-strength variation. Other work tests whether models add value beyond explicit baselines, including sequence models (AlphaGenome, RNA-FM) in noncoding RNA assays. Across ten perturbation-prediction systems, prediction transfer does not imply reliability transfer in single-cell foundation models. Additional preprints find that uncertainty in histology-to-spatial transcriptomics is largely redundant with the predicted mean, and argue that deconvolution misreads disease cell states as abundance changes. The single published paper reports that retaining spliced and unspliced counts as distinct NMF features yields more stable latent structure. The signal data show a rise ratio of 3.11; the items do not establish a cause.",
